Chapter: VectorRasterTest
  cites UI chapter Vector
  cites UI chapter GlyphRasterizer
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 `Vector.codex` had no asserting test before this one: it was cited by
 `forewords/ui-vector`, which prints "UI/Vector OK" and calls nothing. Stage
 2 of the shell refinement wants vector icons so they stop pixelating at
 `ui-wscale` 2, so the chapter gets armed before it gets wired.

 The arms below are in two halves and the second half is the point. What
 `vec-rasterize` DOES is stroke straight segments. What it does not do is
 everything else the chapter's own constructors let a caller build, and the
 way it does not do it is silence: a curve contributes no pixels and no
 diagnostic. These arms record that, so a repair moves a number here instead
 of being believed on inspection.

Section: Body

  vrt-count : VecPath, VecStyle -> Integer
  vrt-count (p) (st) = list-length (vec-rasterize p st)

  vrt-ink : Integer
  vrt-ink = 16711680

  vrt-pen : VecStyle
  vrt-pen = vec-style-stroke vrt-ink 1000

 A horizontal run of four pixels is four pixels, and both ends are drawn.
 An off-by-one at either end is the classic Bresenham defect and it is
 exactly what a count alone cannot see, so the ends are read as well.

  vrt-line : Text
  vrt-line =
    let px = vec-rasterize (vec-line 0 0 3 0) vrt-pen
    in let n = list-length px
    in let first = list-at px 0
    in let last = list-at px (n - 1)
    in "line n=" & show n & " first=" & show (first.vpx-x) & "," & show (first.vpx-y) & " last=" & show (last.vpx-x) & "," & show (last.vpx-y)

  vrt-rect : Text
  vrt-rect =
    let p = vec-rect 0 0 2 2
    in let b = vec-bounds p
    in "rect cmds=" & show (vec-path-length p) & " bounds=" & show (list-at b 0) & "," & show (list-at b 1) & "," & show (list-at b 2) & "," & show (list-at b 3) & " px=" & show (vrt-count p vrt-pen)

 A circle is line segments and not arcs, which is why it draws at all. The
 containment count is the assertion: every pixel of an eight-segment circle
 of radius 50 about (100,100) must land inside the square that circle fits
 in. A rasterizer that dropped a sign or lost the centre would put pixels
 outside it, and a count of pixels alone would not notice.

  vrt-inside : List VecPixel, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  vrt-inside (px) (lo) (hi) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else let p = list-at px i
    in let ok = if p.vpx-x >= lo then (if p.vpx-x <= hi then (if p.vpx-y >= lo then (if p.vpx-y <= hi then 1 else 0) else 0) else 0) else 0
    in vrt-inside px lo hi (i + 1) n (acc + ok)

  vrt-circle : Text
  vrt-circle =
    let p = vec-circle 100 100 50 8
    in let px = vec-rasterize p vrt-pen
    in let n = list-length px
    in "circle cmds=" & show (vec-path-length p) & " inside=" & show (vrt-inside px 50 150 0 n 0) & " of " & show n

Section: Curves

 A bezier and an arc used to build a command, take a slot in the path, move
 the bounds not at all, and rasterize to NOTHING: `vec-raster-cmds` answered
 both with its `is otherwise` arm, so a caller drawing a rounded icon got the
 straight segments and silently lost every curve. Both counts below read 0
 until 2026-08-21.

 They are flattened now, by the same `vec-edges` the fill uses, so the stroke
 and the fill cannot disagree about what a path is. The four lines above this
 one are the control and every one of them is unmoved.

  vrt-curves : Text
  vrt-curves =
    let bz = vec-bezier-to (vec-move-to vec-path-new 0 0) 10 0 20 10 30 10
    in let ar = vec-arc-to (vec-move-to vec-path-new 0 0) 10 10 5 5 1570
    in "bezier cmds=" & show (vec-path-length bz) & " px=" & show (vrt-count bz vrt-pen) & "   arc cmds=" & show (vec-path-length ar) & " px=" & show (vrt-count ar vrt-pen)

 Three of the five style fields are dead on this path. `vec-rasterize` reads
 `vs-stroke-color` and nothing else: it never fills, so `vs-filled` and
 `vs-fill-color` do nothing, and it is one pixel wide, so `vs-stroke-width`
 does nothing.

 The fill case is worse than a no-op and that is why it is read as a COLOUR
 rather than as a flag. `vec-style-fill` sets `vs-stroke-color` to 0, so a
 caller asking for a filled red shape does not get an unfilled red outline,
 it gets a BLACK one.

 `vs-stroke-width` is `Integer between 0 and 1000` and the chapter's
 coordinates are scale-1000 fixed point, so 1000 IS one pixel and the field
 cannot express a wider stroke at all. The bound is enforced rather than
 documentary: `vec-style-stroke vrt-ink 5000` traps at `__narrow` with
 `EXC=06` rather than clamping, measured 2026-08-20. The two widths below
 are both legal, and they are half a pixel and one pixel.

  vrt-style : Text
  vrt-style =
    let fill = vec-style-fill vrt-ink
    in let px = vec-rasterize (vec-rect 0 0 2 2) fill
    in let c = list-at px 0
    in let full = vrt-count (vec-line 0 0 9 0) (vec-style-stroke vrt-ink 1000)
    in let half = vrt-count (vec-line 0 0 9 0) (vec-style-stroke vrt-ink 500)
    in "asked=" & show vrt-ink & " got=" & show (c.vpx-color) & "   w1000=" & show full & " w500=" & show half

Section: Coverage

 `vec-coverage` is the fill, and what it answers is a grid of 0 to 255 rather
 than a list of lit pixels. The three questions it has to get right are
 inside, outside, and the boundary, and only the third distinguishes a
 coverage rasterizer from a stencil.

 The boundary value is asserted as a NUMBER and not as "some partial pixel
 exists". `vec-rect 4 4 8 8` closes at x = 12, so pixel column 12 is the one
 the shape only touches, and it reads 63: four of its sixteen supersamples,
 which is one full supersample COLUMN across the four supersample rows the
 pixel spans. A rasterizer that rounded coverage to a bit, or that sampled at
 the pixel corner rather than across the cell, moves that one number and
 nothing else here.

 It reads 63 rather than 15 because `gr-fill-span-loop` runs to `x1`
 INCLUSIVE, so a filled span is one supersample wider than the path asks for
 on its far edge. Every glyph on the screen is filled by that same span, so
 it is recorded here rather than changed.

 The circle's area is short of the 112 a radius-6 disc encloses because
 `vec-circle` truncates each vertex to a whole path unit before anything is
 scaled: at radius 6 that pulls every vertex inward by up to one unit. It is
 a property of the SHAPE constructor and not of the fill, which is why the
 rectangle above lands where arithmetic says it should. Artwork authored at
 icon sizes rather than at radius 6 pays proportionally less.

  vrt-w : Integer = 16

  vrt-area : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  vrt-area (cov) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length cov then acc / 255
    else vrt-area cov (i + 1) (acc + list-at cov i)

  vrt-fill : Text
  vrt-fill =
    let cov = vec-coverage (vec-rect 4 4 8 8) vrt-w vrt-w
    in "fill rect  in=" & show (vec-coverage-at cov vrt-w 8 8)
       & " out=" & show (vec-coverage-at cov vrt-w 0 0)
       & " edge=" & show (vec-coverage-at cov vrt-w 12 8)
       & " area=" & show (vrt-area cov 0 0)

  vrt-round : Text
  vrt-round =
    let cov = vec-coverage (vec-circle 8 8 6 24) vrt-w vrt-w
    in "fill circle  mid=" & show (vec-coverage-at cov vrt-w 8 8)
       & " corner=" & show (vec-coverage-at cov vrt-w 0 0)
       & " area=" & show (vrt-area cov 0 0)

 The curve arm is a DIFFERENCE against a control that removes the curve and
 keeps everything else, because an absolute area says nothing about whether
 the bezier was the thing that produced it.

  vrt-bowl : VecPath
  vrt-bowl = vec-close (vec-bezier-to (vec-line-to (vec-move-to vec-path-new 2 4) 14 4) 14 14 2 14 2 4)

  vrt-flat : VecPath
  vrt-flat = vec-close (vec-line-to (vec-line-to (vec-move-to vec-path-new 2 4) 14 4) 2 4)

  vrt-curve-fill : Text
  vrt-curve-fill =
    let a = vrt-area (vec-coverage vrt-bowl vrt-w vrt-w) 0 0
    in let b = vrt-area (vec-coverage vrt-flat vrt-w vrt-w) 0 0
    in "curved area=" & show a & " flat area=" & show b & " curve pays=" & (if a > b then "yes" else "NO")

Section: Stroke Coverage

 The stroke answers coverage the way the fill does, so the same three
 questions apply: inside the band, outside it, and the boundary.

 The arm that decides the CONSTRUCTION rather than the arithmetic is the
 crossing. `gr-fill-scanline` is even-odd, so two quads handed to it in one
 edge list cancel where they overlap, and an X would come out with a HOLE at
 exactly the point a pen puts the most ink. `vec-stroke-seg` renders each
 quad on its own and merges by maximum, so the crossing reads full. The
 single segment beside it is the control: it reads full under either
 construction, so it cannot tell them apart and is not evidence about them.

  vrt-pen-w : Integer = 2

  vrt-bar : List Integer
  vrt-bar = vec-stroke-coverage (vec-line 2 8 14 8) vrt-w vrt-w vrt-pen-w

  vrt-cross : VecPath
  vrt-cross =
    let p = vec-line-to (vec-move-to vec-path-new 2 2) 14 14
    in vec-line-to (vec-move-to p 14 2) 2 14

  vrt-stroke : Text
  vrt-stroke =
    "stroke bar  mid=" & show (vec-coverage-at vrt-bar vrt-w 8 8)
    & " far=" & show (vec-coverage-at vrt-bar vrt-w 8 3)
    & " area=" & show (vrt-area vrt-bar 0 0)

  vrt-stroke-cross : Text
  vrt-stroke-cross =
    let cov = vec-stroke-coverage vrt-cross vrt-w vrt-w vrt-pen-w
    in let mid = vec-coverage-at cov vrt-w 8 8
    in "stroke cross  mid=" & show mid
       & " control bar mid=" & show (vec-coverage-at vrt-bar vrt-w 8 8)
       & " ink at crossing=" & (if mid > 0 then "yes" else "NO, EVEN-ODD CANCELLED")

 The arm above says the crossing has ink. It does not say WHY that needed
 building, and a reader has to take the even-odd claim on trust. So the
 rejected construction is built here from the same two quads and rendered in
 ONE `gr-render-edges` call, which is what a single edge list means. It is
 the sabotage kept as a row.

 It reads 47 against the per-quad 255, and NOT 0, which is worth stating
 exactly rather than rounding to "it cancels". The cancelled region is the
 lozenge where the two quads overlap; the centre pixel straddles its
 boundary, so a few of its sixteen supersamples fall outside and survive. The
 claim the row makes is the collapse from 255 to 47 at the point a pen puts
 the most ink, and the arm at 255 beside it is what says the rest of the
 stroke is unharmed and the defect is local to the crossing.

  vrt-k : Integer = vec-super * vec-sub

  vrt-quads : List Edge
  vrt-quads =
    vec-quad (2 * vrt-k) (2 * vrt-k) (14 * vrt-k) (14 * vrt-k) (vec-half-w vrt-pen-w vrt-k)
    & vec-quad (14 * vrt-k) (2 * vrt-k) (2 * vrt-k) (14 * vrt-k) (vec-half-w vrt-pen-w vrt-k)

  vrt-evenodd : Text
  vrt-evenodd =
    let sw = vrt-w * vec-super
    in let cov = gr-downsample-4x (gr-render-edges vrt-quads sw sw 0 sw) sw sw vrt-w vrt-w
    in "one edge list, even-odd  mid=" & show (vec-coverage-at cov vrt-w 8 8)
       & " arm=" & show (vec-coverage-at cov vrt-w 5 5)

 Width is in path units and the band is that many units across, so doubling
 the pen doubles the ink over a straight run. It is asserted as a RATIO
 against the same path rather than as two absolute areas, because the cap
 adds a fixed amount at each end that neither number is about.

  vrt-stroke-width : Text
  vrt-stroke-width =
    let a = vrt-area (vec-stroke-coverage (vec-line 2 8 14 8) vrt-w vrt-w 2) 0 0
    in let b = vrt-area (vec-stroke-coverage (vec-line 2 8 14 8) vrt-w vrt-w 4) 0 0
    in "stroke w2 area=" & show a & " w4 area=" & show b
       & " wider pays=" & (if b > a then "yes" else "NO")

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni vrt-line
    print-line-uni vrt-rect
    print-line-uni vrt-circle
    print-line-uni vrt-curves
    print-line-uni vrt-style
    print-line-uni vrt-fill
    print-line-uni vrt-round
    print-line-uni vrt-curve-fill
    print-line-uni vrt-stroke
    print-line-uni vrt-stroke-cross
    print-line-uni vrt-evenodd
    print-line-uni vrt-stroke-width
  end
